<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Asahan Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1630 CE – 1946 CE (approx.) · capital Tanjung Balai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Malay sultanate on the east coast of Sumatra that grew rich from colonial plantations before its fall in the revolution of 1946.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that Asahan was founded around 1630 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-jalil-of-asahan">Abdul Jalil</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate</a> by a local woman, at the mouth of the Asahan river. For two centuries it was a minor coastal state under Acehnese and later Siak overlordship, trading with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batak">Batak</a> hinterland.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanjungbalai">Tanjung Balai</a> over Malay river communities and collected duties on forest products, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and slaves brought down from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batak">Batak</a> highlands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control from 1865 turned Asahan into part of the plantation belt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-sumatra">East Sumatra</a>, where tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-palm">oil palm</a> estates brought the court large concession revenues.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The sultanate lived on as a self-governing domain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. During the social revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-sumatra">East Sumatra</a> in March 1946, the Malay aristocracy was attacked by revolutionary groups and the sultanate collapsed, its territory passing to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/asahan-sultanate.jpg" alt="Asahan Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dre.comandante · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Asahan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1630 CE – 1946 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tanjung Balai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdul Jalil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Asahan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=asahan-sultanate" aria-label="Asahan Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7433 2065 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Asahan Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7753 2321L7642 2226M7753 2321L7959 2663M7753 2321L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7642 2226h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7753 2321h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7753 2321h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=asahan-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
